<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-matrices-of-homomorphisms" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Matrices of homomorphisms</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
        We introduce a generalized matrix notation. Let <m>R</m> be a commutative ring with unity and <m>A</m> be a right <m>R</m>-algebra. Suppose that <m>\left(M_1,M_2,\ldots,M_n\right)</m> is a sequence of right <m>A</m>-modules. Note that <m>\Hom{A}{M_1}{M_2}</m> is a right <m>A</m>-module.
    </p>
</introduction>
<definition xml:id="def-matrices-of-homomorphism">
    <title>Matrices of homomorphism</title>
    
    
    <statement>
        <p>
            Consider the following collection:
            <me>
                \left[\Hom{A}{M_i}{M_i}\right]=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}\phi_{11}\amp\phi_{12}\amp\cdots\amp\phi_{1n}\\\phi_{21}\amp\phi_{22}\amp\cdots\amp\phi_{2n}\\\vdots\amp\vdots\amp\ddots\amp\vdots\\\phi_{1n}\amp\phi_{n2}\amp\cdots\amp\phi_{nn}\end{bmatrix}:\phi_{ij}\in\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}\right\}
            </me>
            For <m>[\phi_{ij}],[\psi_{ij}]\in\left[\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}\right]</m> we define addition as 
            <me>
                [\phi_{ij}]+[\psi_{ij}]=[\phi_{ij}+\psi_{ij}],
            </me>
            where <m>\phi_{ij}+\psi_{ij}\in\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}</m> is obtained using <m>A</m>-module structure on <m>\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}</m>.
            Similarly, the scalar multiplication is obtained by using <m>A</m>-module structure on <m>\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}</m>:
            <me>
                [\phi_{ij}]\cdot a=[\phi_{ij}\cdot a].
            </me>
            Define the matrix multiplication as follows:
            <me>
                [\phi_{ij}][\psi_{ij}]=\left[\sum_{k=1}^n\phi_{ik}\circ\psi_{kj}\right].
            </me>
            Note that <m>\phi_{ik}\circ\psi_{kj}\in\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}</m> as 
            <me>
                M_j\xrightarrow{\psi_{kj}}M_k\xrightarrow{\phi_{ik}}M_i.
            </me>
            This makes <m>\left[\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}\right]</m> as an <m>R</m>-algebra.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<proposition xml:id="prop-matrices-of-homomorphism-as-endomorphism-of-direct-sum-of-modules">
    <statement>
        <p>
            We have the following isomorphism of <m>R</m>-algebras.
            <me>
                \left[\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}\right]\simeq\End{A}{M_1\oplus M_2\oplus\cdots\oplus M_n}
            </me>
            
        </p>
    </statement>
</proposition>
<proof>
    <p>
        Put <m>M=M_1\oplus M_2\oplus\cdots\oplus M_n</m>. Consider canonical projection homomorphisms <m>\pi_i\colon M\to M_i</m> and canonical inclusion homomorphisms <m>\kappa_j\colon M_j\to M</m>, i.e.,
        <me>
            M_j\xrightarrow{\kappa_j}\to M\xrightarrow{\pi_i}M_i.
        </me>
        Hence we have the following.
        <me>
            \sum_{j=1}^n\kappa_j\circ\phi_i=\unit_{M}\quad\text{and}\quad\pi_i\circ\kappa_j=0\text{ if }i\neq j\quad\text{and}\quad\pi_i\circ\kappa_i=\unit_{M_i}
        </me>
        We define <m>R</m>-algebra homomorphisms
        <me>
            f\colon\left[\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}\right]\to\End{A}{M}\quad\text{by}\quad[\phi_{ij}]\mapsto\sum_{i,j}\kappa_i\circ\phi_{ij}\circ\pi_j
        </me>
        and 
        <me>
            g\colon\End{A}{M}\to\left[\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}\right]\quad\text{by}\quad \phi\mapsto\left[\pi_i\circ\phi\circ\kappa_j\right].
        </me>
        Note that 
        <me>
            M\xrightarrow{\pi_j}M_j\xrightarrow{\phi_{ij}}M_i\xrightarrow{\kappa_i}M
        </me>
        and
        <me>
            M_j\xrightarrow{\kappa_j}M\xrightarrow{\phi}M\xrightarrow{\pi_i}M_i.
        </me>   
        
        We leave it to the reader to check that <m>f,g</m> are indeed <m>R</m>-algebra homomorphisms and they are mutual inverses.
        
    </p>
</proof>
<p>
    As a direct consequence of the above <xref ref="prop-matrices-of-homomorphism-as-endomorphism-of-direct-sum-of-modules"/> we obtain the following corollary.
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor-endomorphism-as-matrices">
    <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>M</m> is a right <m>A</m>-module then,
            <me>
                \End{A}{\oplus_n\,M}\simeq M_n\left(\End{A}{M}\right).
            </me>
            As a particular case
            <me>
                \End{A}{\oplus_n\,A_A}\simeq M_n(A).
            </me>
            
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<corollary xml:id="cor-endomorphism-algebra-as-diagonal-matrices">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>M_1,M_2,\cdots,M_n</m> be right <m>A</m>-modules such that for <m>i\neq j</m>, <m>\Hom{A}{M_j}{M_i}=0</m>. Then,
            <me>
                \End{A}{M_1\oplus M_2\oplus\cdots\oplus M_n}\simeq\End{A}{M_1}\oplus\End{A}{M_2}\oplus\cdots\oplus\End{A}{M_n}.
            </me>
            
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<proof>
    <p>
        This follows from the universal property of direct sum, see <xref ref="prop-universal-property-of-direct-sum"/>.
    </p>
</proof>
</section>